8.3 Verifying outputs
Microsoft’s own guidance (Microsoft Learn — Application card for Microsoft 365
Copilot): users should review and verify responses, and be aware of overreliance —
accepting incorrect or incomplete AI outputs because mistakes are hard to detect.
Microsoft Learn
Preston Gralla, Computerworld, “How to curb hallucinations in Copilot (and other
genAI tools)” (October 23, 2025) advises: “Check Copilot’s citations and follow those
links to see whether they’re trustworthy.” TechBeams Computerworld The article also
recommends never using Copilot output as a final draft and fact-checking each
iteration. Computerworld
Hallucination-defence prompt patterns:
Source-or-silent: “Use only attached documents. If not there, say ‘Not in source.’”
Triple-check (Steve Clayton, Microsoft VP Communications Strategy): “Triple-check
the dates and numbers; cite authoritative sources only.”
Self-review: “After your answer, list the 3 weakest claims and how I would verify
each.”
Confidence flags: “For each claim, mark High/Medium/Low confidence based on
source quality.”
8.4 When NOT to use Copilot
Final regulatory filings, legal opinions, medical/financial certifications. Microsoft: outputs
“must be considered drafts.” Microsoft Learn
Decisions where the cost of error is high without human review.
Sensitive arithmetic without manual verification.
Encrypted/IRM-protected files where extract rights are stripped — Copilot returns empty
or mis-grounded answers.
Teams meeting/chat data with sensitivity labels — those labels aren’t currently
recognised by Copilot and agents Microsoft Learn (per Microsoft Purview
considerations).
Highly specialised vertical workflows (complex underwriting, deal modelling, litigation
strategy) — first drafts only, with mandatory expert review.
Where data permissions are not yet remediated — Copilot will surface anything users
technically have access to.
8.5 Privacy and data handling
Enterprise Data Protection applies to Copilot Chat with M365 Copilot: prompts,
responses, and grounding data are not used to train foundation models.
Saved prompts live in the M365 Substrate Data Store; admins can export them via a
documented PowerShell tool.
Scheduled prompts run in a locked Power Platform “Microsoft 365” environment with a
fixed DLP policy (no premium connectors); Power Platform Admins can inventory all
scheduled prompts. GitHub
Treat Copilot Chat (Web, no M365 Copilot) as you would any external chat tool: no
organisational data.
Recommendations
Stage 1 — Universal baseline (every employee, week 1–2):
1. Save 10–15 personal prompts via the Prompt Gallery.
2. Set up 2 scheduled prompts: morning inbox triage and Friday weekly recap.
3. Bookmark Microsoft’s Prompt Gallery and Volumes 1+2 of these playbooks.
Stage 2 — Team enablement (week 3–6):
1. Build a SharePoint List “Prompt Library” using the PnP JSON formatter; seed with the 25
prompts in §1.4 plus 10–20 department-specific from §4.
2. Pin as a Teams tab.
3. Identify and train one Copilot Champion per team.
4. Run a 30-minute “saved prompts + meta-prompting + scheduled prompts” drop-in.
Stage 3 — Workflow automation (week 6–10):
1. Roll out the 24 standard-connector flows in §5; let teams self-serve from a “Flow
Library” SharePoint page.
2. Pair scheduled Copilot prompts with the flows.
3. Onboard each team to Copilot Notebooks for one ongoing project.
Stage 4 — Department deepening (month 3+):
1. Each department picks 3 of its prompts from §4 and embeds them in SOPs.
2. Track adoption via Copilot Dashboard; intervene where DAU/MAU < 0.3.
3. Quarterly: prune unused prompts, promote high-traffic submissions.
Benchmarks that would change the recommendations:
If Microsoft re-enables a richer Copilot Notebooks custom-instructions experience, shift
more departments to Notebook-based workflows.
If the firm relaxes the “no agents” rule, layer SharePoint Agents on the prompt library to
make it conversational.
If Copilot Dashboard shows assisted hours plateauing at <2 hrs/user/week after 90 days,
restart with mandatory expert-led training.
